          %h2#overview Overview
          %p
            A
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dependent+type"} dependently typed
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/functional+programming+language"} functional programming language
            with applications to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/certified+programming"} certified programming
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proof+assistant"} proof assistant
          %p
            Besides
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rocq"} Rocq
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
            has been implemented (
            %a{:href => "#Brunerie"}> Brunerie
            ).
          %p
            Agda can be compiled to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Haskell"} Haskell
          %h2#variants Variants
          %h3#CubicalAgda Cubical Agda
          %p
            %a{:href => "https://agda.readthedocs.io/en/latest/language/cubical.html"} Cubical Agda
            is a mode of Agda (turned on by the flag
            = succeed ")" do
              %code --cubical
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+type+theory"} cubical type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %p
            Its main difference from CCHM is that instead of an exotype of “cofibrant propositions” it uses the interval itself, replacing cofibrant propositions by statements of the form
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi r
                  %mo ≡
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r \equiv 1
            for some dimension expression
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi r
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} r
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cubical+sets"} cubical sets
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            is not a subobject of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi Ω
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Omega
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Quillen+model+category"} Quillen model category
          %p
            More problematically, to support
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            a la Swan (which are distinct from both cubical “path types” and Martin-Lof “identity types” – the latter sometimes called “jdentity types” to emphasize their definition relative to the J-eliminator) the type of cofibrant propositions must support a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/dominance"} dominance
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi I
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} I
            supports a dominance, but this is not true in De Morgan cubical sets. So the semantics of the entirety of Cubical Agda, with Swan identity types, is unclear. (For this reason, the Cubical Agda library generally avoids using Swan identity types, although Cubical Agda supports them.)
          %p
            Ordinary Martin-Löf
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/identity+types"} identity types
            should, in principle, also be definable in Cubical Agda as an indexed inductive family, with computational behavior as usual for any inductive types in cubical type theory. As of March 2021, however, there is a bug in Cubical Agda that prevents jdentity types from computing correctly.
          %h3#guarded_cubical_agda Guarded Cubical Agda
          %p
            The guarded cubical variant extends cubical Agda to support
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/guarded+recursive"} guarded recursive
            definitions which can be used to formalize
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+guarded+domain+theory"} synthetic guarded domain theory
          %h3#AgdaFlat Agda-flat
          %p
            %code Agda-flat
            is a mode of Agda that implements a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/comonad"} co-
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/idempotent+monad"} monadic
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+operator"} modal operator
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo ♭
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \flat
            (“flat”, following the notation used in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cohesive+homotopy+type+theory"} cohesive homotopy type theory
            as introduced in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/dcct"} dcct
            and type-theorertically developed in
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "cohesive+homotopy+type+theory#Shulman15"} Shulman 15
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+type+theory"} modal type theory
            and hence a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/modal+homotopy+type+theory"} modal homotopy type theory
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/schreiber/show/thesis+Wellen"} Wellen 2017
          %p See:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "https://agda.readthedocs.io/en/v2.6.2.2/language/flat.html"}
                  %code Agda-flat
                  documentation
            %li
              %p
                %a{:href => "http://www.cl.cam.ac.uk/~amp12/agda/internal-universes/code/agda-flat/install.txt"} installation instructions
          %h2#LittleKnownFeatures Little-known features
          %p Listed here are some little-known or undocumented features of Agda that are sometimes useful. Note that undocumented “features” may change without warning; this list is current as of September 2022, Agda v2.6.2.
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                There are a lot of useful documented
                %a{:href => "https://agda.readthedocs.io/en/v2.6.2.2/tools/emacs-mode.html#keybindings"} keybindings
                that you may not be aware of.
              %ul
                %li
                  This appears not to be documented in the manual (although it is in the Emacs docstring): When in a hole, the commands
                  %code C-c C-,
                  and
                  %code C-c C-.
                  can be prefixed with
                  %code C-u C-u
                  to normalize the type of the hole (and the term, in the second case) before displaying them.
            %li
              %p The manual doesn’t document the customizable variables in the Emacs mode. Of particular note are:
              %ul
                %li
                  %code agda-input-user-translations
                  allows you to add new bindings to the Unicode input mode
                %li
                  %code> agda2-highlight-level
                  , when set to
                  = succeed "," do
                    %code interactive
                %li
                  %code agda2-program-args
                  allows you to add command-line arguments to be used every time (e.g. the
                  %code -v
                  options below). This in in addition to the arguments specified by a particular file in the
                  %code OPTIONS
                  line.
              %p
                To change the values of these variables, run
                %code M-x customize-variable RET
                and enter the variable name, change the value, and then “Set and Save”.
            %li
              %p
                The command
                %code -v
                (verbose output) accepts various additional options that are, according to the developers, “documented by their implementation”. These include:
              %ul
                %li
                  %code rewriting.rewrite:50
                  — displays information about attempted uses of rewrite rules.
                %li
                  %code rewriting.match:60
                  — displays information about attempted matches during rewriting.
                %li
                  %code import.chase:2
                  — when compiling imported files, displays a notification when each file is completed in addition to when it is started.
              %p
                Unfortunately, the output produced by these flags appears in the
                %code *Agda debug*
                buffer, which is not visible by default, rather than the standard
                %code AgdaInfo
                buffer. To turn these flags on, you can add (for instance)
                %code -v import.chase:2
                to
                %code agda2-program-args
                via Customization, as above, or to the
                %code OPTIONS
                line of a particular file.
          %h2#minimizing_bugs Minimizing bugs
          %p
            Since Agda has many experimental features under active development, bugs in these features are not uncommon. When
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "https://github.com/agda/agda/issues/new/choose"} reporting a bug
            = succeed "." do
              %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Minimal_reproducible_example"} MRE
            %a{:href => "https://github.com/agda/agda/issues/6067#issuecomment-1236313145"} from the developers
            include
          %ul
            %li
              Enable
              = succeed "," do
                %code --type-in-type
              = succeed "," do
                %code --no-termination-check
              = succeed "," do
                %code --no-positivity-check
              = succeed "," do
                %code --no-projection-like
              %code --no-fast-reduce
            %li Disable unused flags
            %li Remove unused imports
            %li
              Copy definitions from imported modules (other than
              %code Agda.Builtin
              modules)
            %li Delete unused definitions
            %li Turn functions into postulates
            %li Inline functions
            %li Remove clauses from definitions
            %li
              Replace patterns by
              %code _
              if they are not used
            %li Replace types by Set
            %li Delete unused arguments to functions or constructors
            %li Remove dependencies from types
            %li
              Replace (sub)terms by
              %code w/e
              where
              %code w/e : ∀ {ℓ} {A : Set ℓ} → A
              is a postulate
            %li Make implicit arguments explicit
            %li
              Replace wildcards
              %code _
              in terms by their solution
